From: Hans Christian Winther-Sorensen <hc@wsh.no>
Subject: [PATCH] client: store call-method's catch result only when the client asked for it

handle_calls (call-method and interpret) writes the catch result into
args[nargs] whatever nret is. A client that passes nret 0 has no cell
there: args[nargs] is whatever follows its argument array in memory.

OpenBSD 8.0's sparc64 kernel calls the MMU's "map" method that way
(prom_map_phys in sys/arch/sparc64/sparc64/ofw_machdep.c: nargs 7,
nreturns 0, the args structure on the stack, IEEE 1275 gives "map" no
results). The cell after the structure is the function's stack
protector canary, so the store overwrites it with 0 and the check at the
end of prom_map_phys calls __stack_smash_handler from pmap_bootstrap.
panic() then reads curcpu() (the per-CPU page at 0xe0018000, not mapped
yet) and the installed GENERIC kernel stopped right after the loader:

  [ using 1654104 bytes of bsd ELF symbol table ]
  Unimplemented service set-symbol-lookup ([2] -- [0])
  Unhandled Exception 0x0000000000000030
  PC = 0x00000000013e04a8 NPC = 0x00000000013e04ac

(the PC in panic, its format string "smashed stack in %s", called from
__stack_smash_handler, called from prom_map_phys; QEMU -d int and
-d exec). The installer kernel (bsd.rd) is built without the stack
protector, so it booted. "Unimplemented service set-symbol-lookup" is
unrelated: OpenBSD ignores its result.

OpenBSD -current changed these calls to nreturns 1 after 8.0 (ofw_machdep.c
1.39, "did not work with QEMU's OpenBIOS"); kernels up to 8.0 need the
firmware to leave args[nargs] alone. With the store skipped for nret 0
OpenBSD 8.0's installed kernel boots to login. prom_unmap_virt,
prom_free_virt and prom_free_phys ("unmap", "release") use nreturns 0 in
8.0 too.
---
 libopenbios/client.c | 6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libopenbios/client.c b/libopenbios/client.c
index 8b3d582..c4b0ac0 100644
--- a/libopenbios/client.c
+++ b/libopenbios/client.c
@@ -272,8 +272,10 @@ handle_calls(prom_args_t *pb)
 		return 0;
 	}
 
-	/* Store catch result */
-	pb->args[pb->nargs] = val;
+	/* Store catch result, if the client asked for any return values:
+	   with nret 0, args[nargs] is not part of its argument array */
+	if (pb->nret > 0)
+		pb->args[pb->nargs] = val;
 	
 	j = dstackcnt;
 	for (i = 1; i < pb->nret; i++, j--) {
